---
import Cite from '../../components/Cite.astro';
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { codexPage } from '../../data/pages.js';

const page = codexPage('network-partitions');
---
<Article slug={page.slug}>
  <p>
    A network partition splits a system into groups whose messages cannot reach each other. Every member may still be running and
    honest; they just cannot hear the other side. For anything that has to agree, that is a hard problem.
  </p>

  <h2>Consistent or available, not both</h2>
  <p>
    Gilbert and Lynch proved that while a partition lasts, a system cannot both answer every request and keep every answer
    consistent.<Cite page={page} s="cap" /> One side could carry on alone, but then the two sides might make conflicting decisions:
    for ecash, the same note spent twice, once on each side. A federation chooses consistency. It stops deciding until the split heals.
  </p>

  <h2>Only a full quorum can act</h2>
  <p>
    A federation of <em>n</em> guardians acts once <em>t</em> = <em>n</em> − <em>f</em> of them agree.<Cite page={page} s="fmNumPeers" />
    After a split, a side can only keep going if it alone holds <em>t</em> guardians. Two groups of <em>t</em> always overlap, so at
    most one side can ever have that many; usually neither does, and everything waits.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>A federation of 7 guardians (t = 5) split two ways</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Split</th><th scope="col">Can either side sign?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">5 and 2</th><td>Yes, the side of 5</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">4 and 3</th><td>No, the federation waits</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">3, 2 and 2</th><td>No, the federation waits</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <h2>Safe always, live when the network heals</h2>
  <p>
    Byzantine fault-tolerant protocols are built around this. Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance never lets honest replicas disagree,
    whatever the network does, but it only promises progress once messages get through again.<Cite page={page} s="pbft" /> Dwork,
    Lynch and Stockmeyer showed this is the best possible: consensus can stay safe through any delay, and makes progress once the
    network behaves for long enough.<Cite page={page} s="dls" />
  </p>

  <h2>How the game models it</h2>
  <p>
    A Byzantine guardian can cut the network. While it is cut, no withdrawal can be signed, and the link must be repaired before the
    timer runs out: two guardians hold the two glowing relays at once. On Watchtower Peaks the partition is physical. The rope bridges
    between the west and east ranges fall, each range keeps one relay, and the repair takes a guardian on each side. The Academy’s
    Split Network level asks which side of a split, if any, can still sign.
  </p>
</Article>
